  <div class="prose">
    <p>The range is wide on purpose: at 5 ft 10 in it spans 45 lb. BMI was designed to sort large populations into risk groups, and a single reading near a boundary (24.8 vs. 25.1, say) says very little about one person's health.</p>

    <h2>Where BMI misleads</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Muscle.</strong> BMI can't tell muscle from fat. A muscular 5 ft 10 in person weighing 200 lb has a BMI of 28.7, in the "overweight" band, with little body fat. The <a href="body-fat.html">body-fat estimate</a> is a better check in that case.</li>
      <li><strong>Older adults.</strong> Muscle tends to decline with age, so a "normal" BMI can hide a higher fat percentage.</li>
      <li><strong>Different populations.</strong> At the same BMI, risk of type 2 diabetes and heart disease is higher in some groups. A World Health Organization expert consultation suggested lower action points for Asian populations, and the American Diabetes Association recommends diabetes screening for Asian Americans starting at a BMI of 23 rather than 25.</li>
      <li><strong>Children and teens.</strong> Adult cutoffs don't apply. Under 20, BMI is compared with growth charts for the same age and sex, which this calculator doesn't do.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy.</strong> BMI isn't meaningful during pregnancy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Waist size adds what BMI misses</h2>
    <p>Where fat is carried matters as much as how much there is: fat around the abdomen is linked with higher risk than fat elsewhere. 2 simple checks complement BMI:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Waist circumference.</strong> The U.S. National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute treats a waist over 40 inches in men or over 35 inches in women (not pregnant) as a marker of higher risk.</li>
      <li><strong>Waist-to-height ratio.</strong> Keeping your waist under half your height is a guideline used by the UK's NICE. At 5 ft 10 in (70 in), that means a waist under 35 inches.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Measure your waist just above the hip bones, after breathing out. BMI and waist size together are screening tools; a clinician can interpret them alongside blood pressure, blood sugar, cholesterol, and family history.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">BMI questions</h2>
    <details><summary>What is a healthy weight for 5 ft 10 in?</summary><p>A BMI of 18.5 to 24.9 at 5 ft 10 in corresponds to about 129 to 174 lb. BMI is a screening measure, so a clinician's assessment matters more than the exact number.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is BMI accurate for muscular people?</summary><p>No. BMI uses only height and weight, so muscular people can score as overweight with low body fat. A body-fat estimate or waist measurement gives more context.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does BMI work for children?</summary><p>Not with adult categories. For ages 2 to 19, BMI is compared against age- and sex-specific growth charts, usually by a pediatrician.</p></details>
  </section>
